> Does yoga support block layout?


⏺ Great question! Yoga (Facebook's flexbox implementation) is primarily designed for flexbox layout, not traditional block layout.


  What Yoga supports:
  - ✅ Flexbox - display: flex with all flex properties
  - ✅ Absolute positioning - position: absolute
  - ✅ Basic box model - width, height, margin, padding, border
  - ✅ Text sizing - measuring text for layout calculations

  What Yoga doesn't support:
  - ❌ Block layout - no display: block flow
  - ❌ Inline layout - no display: inline
  - ❌ CSS Grid - only flexbox
  - ❌ Float/clear - CSS 2.1 concepts
  - ❌ Table layout - no display: table

  For TOM, this means:
  1. Use flexbox for everything - display: flex is the default
  2. Simulate block layout with flex-direction: column
  3. No need for traditional block layout - flexbox is more powerful anyway

  Example - "Block" layout with Yoga:
  // Instead of display: block
  container.style = {
    display: 'flex',
    flexDirection: 'column'  // Stack children vertically like block
  };

  // Children automatically stack vertically

  This is actually better than traditional block layout because:
  - More predictable sizing
  - Better alignment control
  - Responsive behavior built-in
  - Cross-platform consistency

  So we should embrace flexbox as the primary layout method! Most modern web layouts use flexbox anyway. Traditional block layout
  is becoming less relevant.

  Should I refactor our layout engine to be flexbox-first to match what Yoga will provide?


