⏺ Yes, absolutely! Buttons in a flex container get full flex treatment.

  Current behavior:
  const container = tom.createElement('container'); // display: 'flex' by default
  container.style.gap = 10;
  container.style.justifyContent = 'space-between';
  container.style.alignItems = 'center';

  const button1 = tom.createElement('button'); // display: 'inline-block'
  const button2 = tom.createElement('button'); // display: 'inline-block'

  container.appendChild(button1);
  container.appendChild(button2);
  // Buttons get positioned by Yoga with gap, justification, alignment!

  What happens:
  1. Container uses Yoga flexbox - handles gap, justify-content, align-items
  2. Buttons are Yoga children - positioned by flex layout
  3. Button internals use inline flow - text content positioned with inline algorithm
  4. Two layout systems cooperate - Yoga for structure, inline for content

  So you can absolutely have:
  - Gaps between buttons
  - Justification (space-between, center, etc.)
  - Alignment (flex-start, center, flex-end)
  - Flex grow/shrink if you want buttons to resize
  - Flex direction (row, column, etc.)

  The inline-block display type means "participate in parent's layout as a sized box, but use inline
  layout for my own children." Perfect for UI components in flex containers!

  This gives us the best of both worlds - powerful flex layout for component arrangement, natural text
  flow within components.

