  Raw mode OFF (default):
  - Terminal processes input (handles backspace, Ctrl+C, etc.)
  - Input is line-buffered (wait for Enter)
  - Terminal echo (you see what you type)

  Raw mode ON:
  - Application gets raw keypresses immediately
  - No line buffering (get each key as pressed)
  - No terminal echo (app controls what's displayed)
  - App handles Ctrl+C, arrows, backspace, etc.

  For interactive TUI apps, you typically want raw mode ON so you can:
  - Handle arrow keys for navigation
  - Process Ctrl+C for custom exit handling
  - Get immediate key response (no Enter needed)
  - Control exactly what's displayed

  It's essential for any interactive terminal UI, which is why it belongs in
  TTYRuntime.


